plan Testing
title   Deterministic Stories, checkpoints and property tests
status  proposed
goal
    App authors reproduce application behaviour with isolated Stories, inspect faithful captured
    state and replay generated failures against the production Runtime.
outcomes
    O1  Stories
        A Story runs an app, one machine or one fragment in isolation on the production Runtime, with typed inputs and
        fixtures.

        Story.app(setup, { id, fixtures?, world?, storage?, seed? }) accepts a typed RuntimeSetup.
        Story.machine(machine, { id, input?, context?, fixtures?, world?, seed? }) tests one fresh
        actor; input and context are required exactly when declared and have their declared types.

        Story.fragment(fragment, { id, fixtures?, world?, seed? }) tests a trait or a subroutine
        alone, in an invisible stand-in host whose placement its declaration alone decides. Handing
        the wrong kind of subject to a constructor is a type error.

        Every subject's Effect requirements (a fragment's included) must be met by fixtures, so a
        missing fake is a type error at the constructor. App Stories exercise real App wiring. A
        Story id is nonempty and unique across the configuration; derived exported Stories need
        distinct ids.

        A Story is data: an inert plan of the subject, its options and an ordered list of steps,
        holding inputs and checkpoints and no checks; tests check values with their runner's own
        expect over run.checkpoints and run.frames. Construction, import and discovery start no
        services, actors or fixtures.

        Each chained step returns a new plan; a prefix never changes and nothing in it depends on
        later steps, so branches are plain variables, each run in a fresh Runtime. Scenario commands
        accept no arbitrary callbacks, conditional branches, live handles or raw state overrides.
        Every step has a data form keyed by its method name (Story.Step).

        Steps: send(target?, ...events), together(...inputs), world(...controls), advance (@O2),
        checkpoint(name, { at? }); app Stories add ensure(ref), detach(ref), dispose(ref, { how? }),
        openTab(name, { from? }), closeTab(name), reload(name), dehydrate and hydrate; machine
        Stories `setContext`, dispose() and ensure() (another incarnation of the same identity);
        fragment Stories enter(input?, { subject? }), leave(options?), guards(answers) (the
        stand-in's guard answers, default allow) and reset(subject) for traits, with public events
        and `hostEvents` sendable.

        In a fragment Story, notifications (whenAbandoned's included) are callback records and exits
        are exit records, as in a host. run() is the one execution boundary; it takes no options and
        returns a Promise of a StoryRun with frames, steps, checkpoints, end, evidence, payloads and
        trace.

        One address names a thing everywhere: { actor?, instance?, copy?, operation?, schedule?,
        timer?, key?, tab?, life? }; wire matches, the attempt and lane reads, record filters,
        advance({ next }) and step targets take it, and a request's via and every record carry the
        same fields; no ordinal across actors, hosts or tabs. operation takes a visible handle (a
        definition's O.x, or a fragment's write through Fragment.O.x, as a name only); a fragment's
        read or stream is named by instance and operation name.

        App ensure(ref) attaches one use and detach(ref) ends one (ActorAbsent with none held);
        ensure takes no input (@plan.Runtime.O3). dispose is `disposeActor` and { how: "forget" } is
        forget. `openTab` and reload re-attach the Story's uses; a focused Story holds its subject.

        App send(target, event) targets that exact ref or address; focused send(event) targets its
        actor. send may restore an actor from its record, never create one. Only public events can
        be sent; internal and mapped events require their declared producers.

        Focused setContext(context) replaces the actor's context as a production context update: a
        changed value runs `onContext` as an ordinary turn, an equal value is silent, and the
        initial context is a silent baseline (@plan.Runtime.O5). App Stories cannot inject context.

        Checkpoint names retain exact literal or known-union keys. Duplicate names, widened strings
        and access through unknown literal keys are type errors; other commands preserve the keys.
        defineConfig({ apps: [{ app, stories }], stories? }) discovers configured exported Stories
        (top-level stories have no app) and rejects duplicate or empty ids, foreign setups,
        incompatible machines and lookalike package values before acquiring resources.

        Loading and configuration discovery follow @plan.CommandLine.O6.
    O2  Steps settle; time
        Identical plans, seeds and fake inputs produce identical ordered traces. Each run has fresh
        service state, seeded ids and randomness (each tab seeded from the run seed, tab name and
        open count, so ids never repeat across reloads), and a controlled clock; host time and
        randomness never choose scenario order. Stories execute production behaviour.

        A run never yields to the host between its first step and its cleanup. Every step applies
        its input, then settles on a microtask-only run scheduler (executionMode: "sync"): the
        settle's drain re-queues microtasks until K consecutive hops show no scheduler activity and
        no arrival at anything of Flow's (a mailbox, a gate, the Clock, a connection's or body's
        pull); a settle that does not end within the hop cap is StoryTurnLimit with reason Settle.

        After the last cut, if a commit or read fiber is parked, the run finishes the world (it
        releases held items, closes connections and fires deadlines up to a fixed horizon, settling
        after each, with nothing reaching the evidence); a fiber still parked is HostTimeWait.

        A session or setup-scoped fiber may wait. A sleep of zero or less is a yield. Each step may
        run 10,000 turns per instant; the 10,001st rejects with StoryTurnLimit naming the step and
        the instant, before executing; later steps do not run and partial evidence survives; there
        is no `maxTurns` option.

        Interleaving is fixed: tabs take turns in first-open order, Attempts reach their fakes in
        invocation order, fakes' handlers run in arrival order, and equal instants follow a
        documented total order.

        A Story is checked on the engine its tests run on: its result never depends on host speed,
        but may depend on the engine. Stated limits: a commit on an in-app latch only a later step
        opens is rejected; a real wait under its own timeout, a cancelled fiber's real wait and
        fire-and-forget host work are not caught.

        advance(d) moves time to now plus a finite nonnegative Duration.Input, stopping and settling
        at every deadline on the way, at most 100,000 deadlines (StoryTurnLimit with reason
        Advance). advance({ next: address }) moves to the earliest unfrozen deadline the address
        matches (NoDeadline if none); advance({ to }) to an epoch millisecond at or after now;
        advance(d, { suspended }) models a page in the background, firing the tab's overdue timers.
        together(...inputs) is the only concurrency word: admission is held in every tab while each
        listed send, control or { advance } arrives, then admission resumes and the step settles.

        During cleanup the Clock runs free and cleanup may yield.

        Invalid or backwards movement rejects with InvalidDuration before changing time.
    O3  Fixtures and the world
        Fixtures replace the client's services and a fake server stands in for everything outside the client, so a run
        controls its whole world.

        Fixtures are Effect Layers: the client halves of the world, replacing whole service
        functions while preserving their input, success, failure, cancellation and lifetime
        contracts. Everything outside the client is a fake server, one per run, holding typed truth
        and a log of what it received, shared by every tab and reload, set up before the subject
        starts (the world option), and reached through declared endpoints.

        Fake.server gives run-scoped truth, endpoints and controls; the Story controls the wire with
        four words: hold, release, drop, duplicate. A fake's handle is pure and synchronous and
        decides the result, never the wire; fakes have one result shape and one answer list, route
        tables, a combination rule and a run-scoped server registry; each request carries its
        provenance (via).

        Fake.Lost, Fake.Refused and Fake.Closed are mapped by the transport binding; a crashed
        server is the binding's failure. Fixtures cannot inject operation outcomes directly: every
        ending comes from a fake's answer through the commit's real classification, or from a
        lifecycle step.

        A broken fake rejects the run with StoryFakeError (reason NoRoute, InvalidInput, Crashed,
        UnplacedStream or InvalidRefusal). Unused wire rules are reported (UnusedWireRule). Fixtures
        may depend on setup services and shadow those services.

        Duplicate service Tags among fixtures reject with DuplicateFixture before either conflicting
        provider is acquired; replacement requires an explicit overriding form identifying the
        replaced fixture and replacement Layer. Fixtures providing no service are valid. Missing
        service requirements are type errors; untyped callers fail before actor activation.

        Every run has fresh scoped providers and server truth. Stateful fakes remember writes and
        idempotency results and update that truth before emitting pushes. Story persistence always
        uses Fake.storage, a fresh in-memory store with a purpose per write, FIFO per tab, aborted
        on close, under the scope lock; setup and fixtures never open external storage or inherit a
        live store.

        A tab opened from another (openTab's from) carries storage only. Storage fixtures may seed
        encoded records before bootstrap, but cannot bypass production validation. Acquisition
        failures still trigger @O8 cleanup.
    O4  Frames, checkpoints and StoryFrame
        A run records frames and checkpoints that give faithful captured cuts of its state, so a step can read its own
        effects.

        A frame is one captured cut of the run. A run records a frame after every committed
        publication, a settlement included, and every step ends with a step cut (its latest
        publication frame plus the evidence position, fake truth and wire state at the step's end),
        so a step that publishes nothing still reads its own effects. Frames never collapse.

        A checkpoint names a step cut; checkpoint(name, { at: { settled: O.x } }) names a
        settlement's publication. Every cut captures its full inspection and every frame its work
        and lanes. run.frames keeps at most 10,000 and exposes a non-enumerable truncated; its
        iterator throws FramesTruncated; step cuts and checkpoints are never truncated.

        Each frame names its cause and refers to evidence by sequence position rather than copying
        it. end captures after the last step, before cleanup; it does not mean actors have reached
        final states.

        A cut includes every live actor in that Runtime, including local and nonpersisted actors,
        and no foreign actor. It captures complete published snapshots, one store revision, now,
        work and due times, issues and evidence; partially published context waves never appear as
        complete.

        A checkpoint is read after the run, every read answered from what was captured then: state,
        memory, operations, schedules (machine and fragment Stories), read(target) (an actor's
        snapshot by ref, undefined if absent, or a fake's truth), lane, records (one list, each
        record with tab: { name, open }), inspection (its work replaces pending), entity(...)
        returning { data, predicted, uncertain, unconfirmed } or undefined, attempt and now.

        A read that could name two things is AmbiguousRead; a step addressing an absent subject is
        SubjectAbsent. Field and queue meanings follow @plan.EntityDomains.O2; lanes follow
        @plan.Operations.O6.

        Cuts retain cumulative repairs, skips and diagnostics in accepted order; run.trace also
        includes cleanup events. Truncation is explicit and cannot prove an absent cause, following
        @plan.Inspection.O4. Earlier evidence stays unchanged as execution continues.

        In-process snapshots retain sensitive values faithfully; application opaque values retain
        identity. Reads start no work and cannot resume a Runtime. Exported evidence follows
        @plan.Inspection.O5. Failed capture fabricates no end and preserves earlier checkpoints.

        <StoryFrame frame={frame} tab?> takes a frame and an optional tab and renders real
        components from it without attachment, restoration or operation starts. Actors the run
        captured read their snapshot at that frame.

        An ensuring hook for an actor the frame lacks returns the pre-attach snapshot of
        @plan.React.O2, starts nothing and is reported as a gap of that frame; a lookup for one
        returns null. `useActor` returns its pure pre-attach snapshot. `useNow` returns the frame's
        now.

        Clicks are inert: sends never execute; inside a StoryPlayer (@O9) the player may compare
        them with the Story's next event. The application is rendered below its FlowProvider, since
        StoryFrame supplies the Flow context. Eligibility uses production checks, including denial
        reasons and context. tab selects a tab's cut (@O7). Rendering a frame is not a restore or a
        replay.
    O5  Models and replay (removed)
        Removed: model-based exploration is cut. Flow keeps one runtime; finding unwritten
        sequences stays with propertyTest (@O6) on the real Runtime.
    O6  Reproducible property tests
        A property test generates ordinary Stories from seeded steps and faults and replays any failure against the
        production Runtime.

        propertyTest(story, { steps, properties?, whenQuiet?, seed, runs?, length?, quietWithin? })
        generates ordinary Stories from story on fresh production Runtimes. steps are step data
        forms (every form but checkpoint), generated with pure generators using the supplied seeded
        random source: nonempty readonly sample arrays (uniform, shrinking toward earlier entries)
        or { sample, shrink } objects (a finite ordered shrink sequence).

        Faults are steps:

        - hold, release, fail, delay, reorder, duplicate or lose responses;
        - change server truth and push;
        - disconnect and reconnect;
        - change placements;
        - invalidate;
        - crash and restore;
        - or adopt a supplied domain, all through real service and lifecycle paths, so a lost
          response can leave a committed write unknown.

        Invalid generated steps are skipped and left out of emitted Stories. Properties are plain
        functions, never part of a Story.

        The domain form is domainPropertyOptions(domains, inputs), whose oracle, quietness and
        inconclusive rules belong to @plan.EntityDomains.O4; overlapping domains are tested
        together, with probe subscriptions on every read entry. seed is a safe integer; runs and
        length are positive safe integers. `quietWithin` defaults to 60 seconds of virtual time and
        accepts a finite nonnegative Duration.Input. Pending work at that horizon is inconclusive,
        never pass.

        Every committed publication is checked. After the generated steps, the quiet phase ends the
        world in ordinary steps, which the emitted Story keeps. Results distinguish Pass, Fail and
        Inconclusive and retain seed, completed runs and evidence. `propertyTest` resolves on Pass,
        and rejects with PropertyTestFailed { result } on Fail or Inconclusive, after every run
        completes; a property's defect is PropertyDefect.

        Fail identifies the property and step/publication; turn exhaustion, pending external work, a
        quiet horizon or insufficient evidence is Inconclusive, except that a truncated run is a
        Fail with FramesTruncated. Acquisition, generator and cleanup defects reject as execution
        errors rather than counterexamples.

        Shrinking deterministically tries step chunks, individual steps, then smaller inputs,
        preserving dependencies, input validity and step pairs. Each candidate runs fresh and is
        retained only if the same property fails. Stop at 1,000 candidates or no smaller failure;
        report the bound.

        Re-run the final failure and emit a static Story with literal inputs, controls and seed.
        Repeated replay produces identical traces. Export redaction identifies omissions that
        prevent the printed projection alone from reproducing the failure.
    O7  Isolation, hydration and lazy features
        Stories are isolated per run, scope and request, and model a reload or a second tab as separate tabs.

        Run, scope and request isolation follows @plan.Runtime.O7. Tests use no browser storage or
        live state from another run.

        App Stories model reload and a second tab as tabs:

        - independent Runtimes of the Story's setup sharing that run's fake servers and
          Fake.storage. openTab(name, { from? }) constructs and readies one;
        - reload(name) reloads one;
        - closeTab(name) ends it abruptly, with no final flush, like a closed or crashed page, and
          releases its scope ownership;
        - remote requests in transit survive `closeTab`, local writes abort. send, ensure and
          checkpoint reads take an optional tab (default "main");
        - records carry tab: { name, open }.

        A tab opened while another holds the scope runs without persistence (@plan.Persistence.O7).
        Tabs share nothing else; there is no cross-tab coordination. A clean Runtime shutdown with
        queued work is a test of Flow's own policy suite.

        App dehydrate(label, { within, maxBytes? }) captures through production server settle,
        moving time as advance does, deadline by deadline, until quiet or within. It stores
        run.payloads[label] and leaves the scenario Runtime available.

        Labels follow checkpoint key rules. hydrate(source, label?) accepts a payload or an
        independently executed source Story; a hydration source runs in the destination's world, on
        the run's clock, may not set world, and its requests carry via.tab: { name: "server", open
        }. Leading hydrate runs before bootstrap and storage restore; later hydrate models
        navigation.

        Payload contents follow @plan.ServerRendering.O3, validation and installation
        @plan.ServerRendering.O4, precedence @plan.ServerRendering.O5 and isolation
        @plan.ServerRendering.O6. Source cycles and missing labels reject before execution; failures
        identify the source or destination boundary. Checkpoints expose hydration evidence and
        serverView(label) for first-render comparison.

        Lazy ensure and focused construction adopt supplied definitions at the chosen step without
        moving time or invoking a module loader, following @plan.LazyFeatures.O2. Checkpoints
        expose adopted entries; unrelated dormant records remain dormant. Real module loading is
        exercised by production host tests.
    O8  Cleanup and failure evidence
        Every run finalizes the resources it owns, whether it succeeds, fails or only partly acquires them.

        A run takes no cancellation signal: it never yields to the host, so nothing could call abort
        during one, and a runner's timeout reports only when the run ends; the hop cap, the turn
        limit and the deadline limit bound it. Success, execution failure and partial acquisition
        all finalize owned resources.

        Cleanup attempts each finalizer once even when another fails; fixtures cannot suppress it.
        It runs in a fixed order: stop admission, dispose actors consumers before providers, dispose
        the Runtime, then release fixture and host scopes last. Production disposal guarantees
        follow @plan.Runtime.O6.

        run() rejects on a real Story error or a broken fake, never on a value: StoryTurnLimit,
        HostTimeWait, StoryInvalidCommand (ActorAbsent, AlreadyEntered, NotEntered, TabOpen,
        TabClosed, NothingHeld, NoDeadline, NoConnection), InvalidDuration, SubjectAbsent,
        AmbiguousRead, FramesTruncated, StoryFakeError, a failed acquisition or a cleanup failure.

        After a resolve every declared checkpoint is present and typed. The earliest recorded error
        is primary, ordered by the step where a fiber parked, and later ones are listed; otherwise
        the first cleanup failure is primary.

        Cleanup diagnostics retain their order, and cleanup failure never yields success. The full
        Cause remains available in process through cleanup; projection follows @plan.Diagnostics.O3
        and never serializes Cause or private recovery authority.

        Rejection is a FlowError carrying readonly Story evidence: frames up to the failing
        boundary, completed checkpoints, captured end and payloads if any, failing step index or
        bootstrap/capture/cleanup boundary, primary and cleanup diagnostics, trace and truncation,
        and last accepted and drained evidence positions. Direct and CLI runs agree on results and
        retained evidence.
    O9  Story playback
        A recorded run can be played, stepped and scrubbed without executing anything again.

        <StoryPlayer run={run}> plays a run's frames over time under a floating overlay, built only
        from StoryFrame; <StoryPlayer presentation={presentation}> first records the presentation's
        own Story once on mount. Recording is the only execution: playing, stepping, scrubbing and
        restarting render existing frames, so moving backwards is a lookup, never a re-execution.

        Re-recording happens only on mount or on request, never from a cached run. Controls are
        play, pause, next and previous (by frame, step or scene), scrub, speed, restart and inspect,
        which shows the frame's cause, gaps and the run's evidence for it, or evidence unavailable
        beyond the trace's retained window.

        definePresentation(story, { scenes, pacing? }) describes how to present one Story's run. A
        scene names a checkpoint of that Story (a type error otherwise) and may carry a caption, a
        hold and a route string for the host's router; it applies from its frame until the next
        scene. pacing has speed, `maxWait` and `minDwell`.

        A presentation holds no event callbacks; the Story stays the only source of events. The
        player highlights the control whose click would send the Story's next event, comparing the
        addressed event. A closed tab renders nothing and reports the gap. Fragment-alone Stories
        are not playable (a type error). Route changes run host code; only Flow work is inert.
verification
    V1  Type errors prove exact inputs, context, fixtures, checkpoint keys and the checkpoint
        reads' types.
    V2  Stories prove inert construction, branch isolation, fixture overrides and fresh storage.
    V3  Tests prove every step settling and the instant order, with the host-primitive matrix,
        an always-on sentinel and K doubling, and rejection before turn 10,001.
    V4  Tests prove complete passive cuts, publication frames, step cuts and captured reads,
        stable earlier evidence, and StoryFrame rendering with gaps, inert clicks and the frame's
        time.
    V5  Removed with @O5 (T1; C77).
    V6  Property tests prove seeded replay, validated steps, Pass, Fail and Inconclusive judging,
        FramesTruncated, and shrinking.
    V7  Stories prove isolated hydration evidence, exact-step lazy adoption without loading,
        reload by reload, closeTab and openTab, and a second tab running without persistence.
    V8  Tests prove cleanup failures and direct/CLI partial-evidence parity.
    V9  Player tests prove playback starts no Flow work, scrubbing never re-executes, and
        presentation scenes reject unknown checkpoints.
questions
    Q1  What measured quiet horizon and shrink budget should replace the initial defaults?
        Retain 60 virtual seconds and 1,000 shrink candidates until measurements justify a change;
        a budget change must never turn incomplete evidence into pass.
    Q2  Deferred to build time
        Fixture composition mechanics and override syntax. Property control identifiers and
        literal encoding. Checkpoint container freezing and private name lookup. Cleanup phase
        implementation. Discovery identity encoding. Evidence artifact codecs and host coverage.
        The settle constant K and the hop cap.
